{"type": "message", "channelId": "pva-published-engine-direct", "id": "dd6ed11e-d517-4712-bf7f-a2237dd4a8de", "timestamp": "2026-10-06T16:37:07.305591Z", "from": {"id": "Default-a3321a7a-958c-4f4a-ad4f-f4d9c193c977/d5ede7cc-d9bf-f111-aaaf-00224835685d", "name": "cr350_PWSharepointAgentOpt1", "role": "bot"}, "conversation": {"id": "4638f6ff-8044-4cb9-983e-4e26bc98d1b7"}, "recipient": {"id": "df390249-9cf9-461e-a7d0-bb3a3f6c9584", "aadObjectId": "df390249-9cf9-461e-a7d0-bb3a3f6c9584", "role": "user"}, "textFormat": "markdown", "membersAdded": [], "membersRemoved": [], "reactionsAdded": [], "reactionsRemoved": [], "text": "In “Definable Convolution and Idempotent Keisler Measures III: Generic Stability, Generic Transitivity, and Revised Newelski’s Conjecture,” Artem Chernikov, Kyle Gannon, and Krzysztof Krupiński study idempotent measures and the structure of convolution semigroups of measures over definable groups. They introduce and analyze the property of generic transitivity, showing that it is sufficient and necessary for developing a localized stable group theory around generically stable types, and they extend results from types to generically stable Keisler measures. They also classify idempotent generically stable measures in abelian groups as unique translation-invariant measures on type-definable fsg subgroups. [1]

The conjecture revisited is Newelski’s conjecture in its revised form. Specifically, they prove the revised Ellis group conjecture of Newelski for countable NIP groups and establish the revised Newelski conjecture asserting that, for NIP groups, the τ-topology on the relevant Ellis group component is Hausdorff. [1]

